Changelog: 15-10-2026 22:51:19

Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 22:35:35

Game Mode shortcuts are named with the system first, "[PS2] Title" (owner, 15-10-2026), so Steam's menus, such as the
recent games in the tray icon's menu, tell a game apart from the same title on Steam or on another system. A shortcut
in Steam with another name than the app gives it now, as one from before, turns Update Game Mode on; the update
renames it, keeps its play time, and removes the artwork under its old Steam number (core's write_shortcuts).

Changelog: 15-10-2026 21:03:51

images/bc250-pcsx2-manager-128.png: the 128 px version of the icon, scaled from the 512 px file, so the app's images
folder has both sizes like the other apps. It is the source for the portal's tile icon.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 18:26:02

A nightly channel (owner's decision 15-10-2026: PCSX2's nightly builds are mostly stable). The first step has a
Nightly builds box: ticked, Install downloads PCSX2's newest nightly (its GitHub pre-releases, almost one a day) and
checks it against the SHA-256 GitHub computed for that build (the asset's digest in the API), which proves the
download is whole, not that the build was tried on the board; the window says so, says when a newer nightly is out,
and the newer-stable notice stays on the stable channel. Unticked (the default), Install puts the tested stable
release back. The installed build's size is written beside it (.size), so the app notices PCSX2 updating itself on
either channel. The channel is remembered in the settings.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 17:35:00

New app, PCSX2 Manager (0.1.0, decided 15-10-2026 by the owner): PS2 games on the BC-250 with PCSX2, built as the
RPCS3 Manager is. Install downloads the PCSX2 2.8.2 Linux AppImage (the stable release, not a nightly) from its
GitHub release and checks it against the SHA-256 the app was tried with; Remove takes it out and keeps PCSX2's own
folder (~/.config/PCSX2: the BIOS, memory cards, saves, settings). The BIOS step counts the dumps in PCSX2's bios
folder and opens it, so the dump from your own console can be copied in; choosing the one to use stays in PCSX2's
own window. The app finds the games in the chosen folders (an ISO read for its SYSTEM.CNF: the serial, as PCSX2
shows it, and the disc's version; CHD, CSO, ZSO and GZ listed under their file names) and puts the ticked ones in
Steam for Game Mode; a PS2 disc carries no pictures, so the artwork is drawn, the game's title and "PCSX2 Manager"
on a dark background, until the owner decides the real artwork. The Game Mode shortcuts run
~/.local/bin/bc250-pcsx2-run (-nogui -fullscreen --), which reads a real controller itself while one is connected
and opens the windows through XWayland when Steam starts a game on a Wayland desktop, so Steam's overlay works. The
app says when PCSX2 published a newer stable release, and says so when PCSX2 updated itself in its own window
(Install puts the pinned release back); it never installs one it was not tried with. The window icon is a
placeholder until the owner's images are ready.
